Actually, wait — maybe the filter checks the decoded value for `..`. In that case, no encoding tricks will work. Unless there's a double-decode situation (the app calls urldecode again on top of PHP's decoding). Let's try `%252e%252e%252f`: after PHP's first decode it becomes `%2e%2e%2f` (no literal `..` so the filter passes), and if the app urldecodes again → `../`. Worth trying.

